# Project Structure

All application code lives under **`src/`**. Use the Next.js **`src/app/`** App Router for routing (standard file conventions) and keep route files thin.

## Which layout this project uses

Two layouts are supported. Detect the one in use before creating files — the project's `AGENTS.md` (**Project Structure**) is authoritative:

| Layout | Signal | Product code goes in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Feature-driven** | `src/features/` exists | `src/features/<feature>/`, cross-feature code in `src/shared/` |
| **Flat** | no `src/features/` — domain folders directly under `src/` | `src/<domain>/` (`utils/`, `hooks/`, `components/`, …) |

Never mix layouts in one project, and never introduce the other layout without being asked.

Both layouts share every rule below about `src/` as the application root, `src/app/` usage, and promotion of reused code.

## `src/` is the application root

Next.js supports an optional [`src` directory](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/src-folder). This project **always** uses it.

- ✅ Application code: `src/app/` plus the layout's folders (`src/features/` + `src/shared/`, or `src/<domain>/`)
- ✅ `@/` alias resolves to `src/` (e.g. `@/features/auth` → `src/features/auth`, `@/utils/group-by` → `src/utils/group-by`)
- ❌ Do **not** create a root-level `app/` folder — use `src/app/` only
- ❌ Do **not** put features, shared modules, or utilities at the repo root

Project root keeps framework, tooling, and — when the project has them — **project docs**:

```
project-root/
├── docs/                   # Project knowledge — architecture, features, ADRs (when present)
├── agents/                 # Agent coding rules
├── public/                 # Static assets (Next.js convention)
├── src/                    # All application code
│   ├── app/
│   └── …                   # features/ + shared/, or domain folders
├── next.config.ts
├── package.json
└── tsconfig.json
```

Optional Next.js entry files also belong under `src/` when used:

- `src/middleware.ts`
- `src/instrumentation.ts`

## Flat layout — `src/<domain>/`

Used by projects without `src/features/`. Reusable code goes in the matching domain folder:

| Kind | Location |
| --- | ---- |
| Pure helpers, formatters, mappers | `src/utils/` |
| React hooks | `src/hooks/` |
| Shared TypeScript types | `src/types/` |
| Shared React components | `src/components/` |
| Third-party wrappers, integrations | `src/lib/` |
| App configuration | `src/config/` |
| Shared constants | `src/constants/` |
| Validation schemas | `src/schemas/` |
| React contexts | `src/contexts/` |
| State reducers | `src/reducers/` |
| HTTP clients, API helpers | `src/http/` |
| Global styles, design tokens | `src/styles/` |

- ✅ Good:

  ```
  src/utils/group-by.ts
  src/hooks/use-form-state/use-form-state.ts
  src/types/optional.ts
  src/styles/globals.css
  ```

- ❌ Bad:

  ```
  src/app/group-by.ts
  src/app/use-form-state.ts
  src/app/globals.css
  ```

When logic is reused outside one route, move it to the matching `src/<domain>/` folder.

## Feature-driven layout

```
src/
├── app/                    # Next.js App Router — routes only (thin)
├── features/               # Product features (vertical slices)
│   ├── auth/
│   ├── billing/
│   └── dashboard/
├── shared/                 # Cross-feature shared code
│   ├── components/
│   ├── hooks/
│   ├── utils/
│   ├── lib/
│   ├── types/
│   ├── config/
│   ├── constants/
│   ├── schemas/
│   ├── contexts/
│   ├── reducers/
│   ├── http/
│   └── styles/
└── mocks/                  # Test mocks (optional)
```

## `src/features/<feature>/` — feature modules (feature-driven development)

**Features are the primary unit of organization.** Each feature is a self-contained vertical slice that can grow a **deep internal tree** — add subfolders as needed; there is no fixed cap on nesting.

Use **kebab-case** for feature folder names (`auth`, `billing`, `team-settings`).

See [feature-driven-development.md](../../docs/architecture/feature-driven-development.md) for the full guide and billing example tree.

### Common feature folders

Create only what the feature needs — not every folder is required:

| Folder | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `components/` | Feature UI — nest by screen/flow (`components/checkout/`, …) |
| `hooks/` | Feature-specific React hooks |
| `actions/` | Server actions |
| `queries/` | TanStack Query keys, fetchers, hooks |
| `mutations/` | TanStack Query mutations |
| `services/` | Business logic orchestration (no JSX) |
| `lib/` | Feature-specific integrations (e.g. `lib/payment/`) |
| `utils/` | Pure helpers — one exported function per file |
| `schemas/` | Zod validation |
| `types/` | Feature types |
| `constants/`, `config/` | Feature constants and static config |
| `contexts/`, `reducers/` | Client state scoped to this feature |
| `emails/` | Feature-owned templates/triggers (optional) |
| `api/` | Internal API/route helpers |
| `__tests__/` | Tests — mirror internal structure |
| `index.ts` | **Public API** — only exports consumed outside the feature |

### Example — large feature

```
src/features/billing/
├── components/
│   ├── checkout/
│   ├── history/
│   └── credits/
├── hooks/
├── queries/
├── mutations/
├── actions/
├── services/
├── lib/payment/
├── schemas/
├── types/
├── utils/
├── index.ts
└── __tests__/services/
```

### Spec layer folders → features first

When the product spec mentions root `/lib`, `/utils`, `/hooks`, `/components`, `/schemas`:

1. Default to `src/features/<feature>/<folder>/`
2. Promote to `src/shared/<folder>/` only when **two or more** features need the code

- ✅ Good:

  ```
  src/features/billing/components/checkout/checkout-form.tsx
  src/features/billing/queries/get-credit-balance.ts
  src/features/billing/services/process-webhook-event.ts
  src/features/billing/lib/payment/abacatepay.ts
  src/features/billing/index.ts
  ```

- ❌ Bad:

  ```
  src/components/checkout-form.tsx           # belongs in a feature
  src/hooks/use-credit-balance.ts            # billing-specific → feature
  src/lib/payment/abacatepay.ts              # billing-owned → feature/lib
  src/app/(app)/billing/actions.ts           # business logic in route tree
  ```

### Feature boundaries

- Import another feature only through its public barrel: `@/features/auth`
- Do **not** reach into another feature's internals (`@/features/auth/components/...`)
- Prefer relative imports within the same feature
- When code is used by **one** feature, keep it in that feature
- When code is used by **two or more** features, move it to `src/shared/`

## `src/shared/` — cross-feature code

Use `src/shared/` for code with no single feature owner — design-system primitives, generic hooks, third-party wrappers, global config, and global styles.

| Kind | Location |
| --- | --- |
| Shared UI primitives | `src/shared/components/` |
| Shared hooks | `src/shared/hooks/` |
| Shared helpers | `src/shared/utils/` |
| Third-party wrappers | `src/shared/lib/` |
| Shared types | `src/shared/types/` |
| App configuration | `src/shared/config/` |
| Shared constants | `src/shared/constants/` |
| Shared validation | `src/shared/schemas/` |
| React contexts | `src/shared/contexts/` |
| State reducers | `src/shared/reducers/` |
| HTTP clients | `src/shared/http/` |
| Global styles | `src/shared/styles/` |

- ✅ Good:

  ```
  src/shared/components/button/index.tsx
  src/shared/utils/tailwind-cn.ts
  src/shared/lib/sentry/index.ts
  src/shared/styles/globals.css
  ```

## `src/app/` — Next.js App Router (routing only)

`src/app/` is the App Router root. Follow [Next.js file conventions](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions) — do not invent custom route file names or folder patterns.

### Allowed route files

Use only Next.js special files inside the route tree:

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `page.tsx` | Route UI (required for a URL segment) |
| `layout.tsx` | Shared UI wrapper for a segment and its children |
| `loading.tsx` | Loading UI (Suspense boundary) |
| `error.tsx` | Error UI (error boundary) |
| `not-found.tsx` | Not found UI for a segment |
| `template.tsx` | Re-mounted layout variant |
| `default.tsx` | Parallel route fallback |
| `route.ts` | Route Handler (API endpoint) |

Metadata and static route files (`icon`, `opengraph-image`, `robots.ts`, `sitemap.ts`, etc.) follow the same Next.js conventions under `src/app/`.

### Route organization

Use Next.js routing features as documented:

- **Route groups**: `(marketing)`, `(dashboard)` — organize without affecting the URL
- **Dynamic segments**: `[id]`, `[...slug]`
- **Parallel routes**: `@modal`, `@sidebar`
- **Intercepting routes**: `(.)photo`, `(..)photo`
- **API routes**: `src/app/api/<path>/route.ts`

### Thin pages — delegate outward

Route files compose code that lives outside the route tree; they do not own business logic.

- Import UI, actions, and data helpers from `@/features/...` and `@/shared/...` (feature-driven) or `@/components/...`, `@/utils/...` (flat)
- Pass route params, search params, and server data into those components
- Global CSS is imported in the root layout from the project's styles folder (`@/shared/styles/globals.css` or `@/styles/globals.css`)

- ✅ Good:

  ```tsx
  // src/app/layout.tsx
  import '@/shared/styles/globals.css'

  export default function RootLayout({ children }: Readonly<{ children: React.ReactNode }>) {
    return (
      <html lang="en">
        <body>{children}</body>
      </html>
    )
  }
  ```

  ```tsx
  // src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx
  import { LoginForm } from '@/features/auth'

  export default function LoginPage() {
    return <LoginForm />
  }
  ```

  ```typescript
  // src/app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts
  import { handleStripeWebhook } from '@/features/billing'

  export async function POST(request: Request) {
    return handleStripeWebhook(request)
  }
  ```

- ❌ Bad:

  ```
  app/page.tsx                              # root app/ — must be src/app/
  src/app/(auth)/login/login-form.tsx       # feature UI in the route tree
  src/app/utils/format-date.ts              # utilities in app/
  src/app/hooks/use-auth.ts                 # hooks in app/
  src/app/globals.css                       # global styles in app/ — use shared/styles
  src/app/(auth)/login/page.ts              # wrong extension — use page.tsx
  src/app/dashboard/DashboardPage.tsx       # custom page file name — use page.tsx
  ```

Do **not** add standalone utilities, hooks, schemas, components, or global styles under `src/app/`.

## Import paths

- `@/features/<feature>` — feature public API (barrel), feature-driven layout
- `@/shared/<domain>/...` — shared cross-feature code, feature-driven layout
- `@/<domain>/...` — domain folders, flat layout
- `@/mocks/...` — test mocks
- Prefer `@/` when the relative path goes up more than one folder level

## Promoting code

**Feature-driven:**

1. Start new product behavior in `src/features/<feature>/`
2. If a second feature needs the same code, move it to the matching `src/shared/<domain>/` folder
3. Update imports; keep feature barrels exporting only that feature's public surface

**Flat:**

1. Start route-local code next to the route in `src/app/`
2. When it is reused outside that route, move it to the matching `src/<domain>/` folder
3. Update imports to the `@/<domain>/...` alias

